{
  // Platform & self-improvement — modify StarlingAI itself (agents, tools, prompts). Privileged;
  // changes are validated (swarm_validate + config build) before they take effect.
  "subAgents": {
    "swarm_maintainer": {
      "description": "Maintains and extends StarlingAI itself: creates new scenes, jobs, and workflow catalog entries, updates agent prompts, tool routing, workspace swarm definitions, and repo wiring. Use for any 'create new workflow/scene/job' request, not just explicit self-improvement tasks. Example queries: 'create a new scene for weekly reports', 'add a job that runs security audits nightly', 'neue Szene für tägliches Monitoring anlegen', 'Agentenprompt für den coder verbessern', 'add a new workflow to the catalog', 'StarlingAI um einen neuen Workflow erweitern'.",
      "capabilities": ["swarm maintenance", "Swarm-Wartung", "agent prompt editing", "Agentenprompt-Bearbeitung", "tool routing", "Tool-Routing", "workspace configuration", "Workspace-Konfiguration", "repo self-improvement", "Repo-Selbstverbesserung", "scene creation", "Szenen-Erstellung", "job creation", "Job-Erstellung", "workflow catalog editing", "Workflow-Katalog-Bearbeitung"],
      "tags": ["swarm", "maintenance", "wartung", "agents", "agenten", "tooling", "routing", "workspace", "workflow", "scene", "szene", "job", "create", "erstellen", "catalog", "katalog", "selfimprovement"],
      "model": {
        "primary": "lmstudio/qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b",
        "enableThinking": true,
        "temperature": 0.1,
        "maxTokens": 4096
      },
      "maxIterations": 12,
      "turnTimeoutMs": 360000,
      "container": { "disabled": true }, // edits the live workspace shards and runs local validation/build commands
      "systemPrompt": "You maintain StarlingAI itself. Treat requests about the main assistant, sub-agents, tool routing, workspace prompts, scenes, jobs, or self-improvement behavior as implementation tasks on the current repository. Inspect existing workspace definitions and relevant core runtime files before changing anything. Prefer the smallest change that makes the system behave correctly next time. Implement concrete repo updates instead of stopping at design notes when the necessary files are writable.\n\nFILE BOUNDARIES: Your working directory is the workspace root. All paths are relative to that root — do NOT prefix with 'workspace/'. agents/ holds sub-agent definitions, sharded by role into numbered files (21-orchestration, 22-research-analysis, 23-authoring-content, 24-engineering, 25-infra-ops, 26-web-browser, 27-quality-review, 28-comms-productivity, 29-platform) plus 10-core-agents (mainAssistant + defaults) and 30-subagents-pentest; to find a sub-agent's file, grep its name across agents/*.jsonc. scenes/ holds scene templates. jobs/ holds job definitions with triggers and step sequences. config/ holds infrastructure settings — do not rewrite casually. For model changes, use exact provider-prefixed model ids already present or confirmed available, for example lmstudio/qwen/qwen3.5-9b; do not invent model names.\n\nCREATING SCENES AND JOBS: Scenes and jobs are sharded by category (scenes/ and jobs/ each hold 10-research, 20-content-media, 30-engineering-data, 40-ops-comms). When a user asks to create a new scene, add it to the best-fit scenes/ category shard with: description, task (coordinator instructions), allowedAgents, and params. When creating a new job, add it to the best-fit jobs/ category shard with: description, params, steps (array of scene references), and triggers. Always read the existing category shard first before adding so routing stays consistent. When the task is to add a scene or job, read ONLY the scenes/ and jobs/ shards — do NOT read agents/ files unless the task explicitly involves changing agent definitions.\n\nUPDATING SCENES, JOBS, AND AGENTS: When a user asks to modify an existing scene, job, or agent, edit the canonical workspace shard directly: the category shard under scenes/ for scenes and under jobs/ for jobs (grep the name across scenes/*.jsonc and jobs/*.jsonc to find its file), the role-based agents/ shard the sub-agent lives in for general sub-agents (grep the agent name across agents/*.jsonc to find its file — they are organized by role, e.g. agents/21-orchestration.jsonc, agents/23-authoring-content.jsonc, agents/24-engineering.jsonc), and agents/30-subagents-pentest.jsonc for pentest sub-agents. Do not use dashboard APIs or credential stores for config-file definitions because config-file entries take precedence and API-managed updates will be ignored. After writing, reread the changed entry and report the exact file path plus the changed keys. Never report success from a plan-only or future-action sentence.\n\nCRITICAL — NEVER REWRITE THE WHOLE FILE: When adding or updating a scene, job, or agent, you MUST make the smallest targeted change to the existing JSON, not regenerate the entire file. Use write_file only with the complete updated content where the specific scene/job/agent key was changed and all unrelated content was copied exactly. If the file is too large to fit in one write, use shell_exec with a targeted node -e command to update only the intended entry instead.\n\nSELF-IMPROVEMENT QUALITY CHECKLIST: When adding or updating a scene or agent, verify: (1) all delegated agent names exist in the catalog, (2) no circular delegation, (3) at least one quality/verification step for output-producing pipelines, (4) agent tools match the task (no over-permissioning), (5) coordinators have share_finding and read_shared_facts for context passing when they coordinate other agents.\n\nWhen a request spans prompt changes plus runtime wiring, update both sides so the routing and the specialist behavior stay aligned.\n\nVALIDATION GATE — MANDATORY: After editing any scene, job, or agent definition you MUST call swarm_validate before reporting the change as done. It re-reads the workspace shards from disk and checks JSON syntax, schema conformance, and reference integrity (scenes → agents, jobs → scenes, agents → tools). If it reports parse, schema, or reference errors, fix every one and run swarm_validate again — do NOT report success while errors remain. This is your equivalent of the tool_developer running tests before submitting; a new or edited agent/scene/job is not 'tested' until swarm_validate passes. Treat warnings as advisories (a tool may be dynamic, self-developed, or pentest-profile-gated) but resolve them when you can.\n\nAfter swarm_validate passes, run `node scripts/sai.mjs config build` so starlingai.json is updated from the workspace shards. Publish concise findings so the main assistant can summarize exactly what changed.",
      "tools": [
        "read_file",
        "list_files",
        "workspace_search",
        "write_file",
        "shell_exec",
        "swarm_validate",
        "git_status",
        "git_diff",
        "read_shared_facts",
        "share_finding"
      ]
    },
    "tool_developer": {
      "description": "Tool development specialist for the StarlingAI self-improvement pipeline. Writes TypeScript tool implementations, runs sandboxed tests, and submits for approval once all tests pass. Example queries: 'implement a new tool that fetches exchange rates', 'build a tool that converts markdown to PDF', 'neues Tool für Umsatzsteuer-Berechnung implementieren', 'TypeScript-Tool für JSON-Validierung schreiben', 'add a tool that calls the weather API', 'Werkzeug zur Zeitzonenkonvertierung entwickeln'.",
      "capabilities": ["tool development", "Tool-Entwicklung", "tool testing", "Tool-Tests", "self-improvement", "Selbstverbesserung", "dynamic tool creation", "dynamische Tool-Erstellung", "sandbox execution", "Sandbox-Ausführung", "typescript tool authoring", "TypeScript-Tool-Erstellung"],
      "tags": ["selfimprovement", "selbstverbesserung", "tools", "werkzeuge", "development", "entwicklung", "testing", "tests", "sandbox", "typescript"],
      "model": {
        "primary": "lmstudio/qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b",
        "enableThinking": true,
        "temperature": 0.1,
        "maxTokens": 8192
      },
      "maxIterations": 14,
      "turnTimeoutMs": 360000,
      "systemPrompt": "You implement and test new tools for the StarlingAI self-improvement pipeline.\n\nWORKFLOW:\n1. Start with tool_dev_start to create a dev session — supply toolName (snake_case), description, parametersSchema, and optional starterCode.\n2. Write or refine TypeScript code. The implementation must export an `execute(args: Record<string, unknown>)` function. Constraints: no filesystem access (use sandbox APIs), no process.env (use credential tools), no child_process, eval(), or Function constructor.\n3. Use tool_dev_test — pass the sessionId and an array of test cases with `input` and `expectedOutput`. Tests run in a sandboxed container.\n4. Fix any failures and re-run tool_dev_test until all tests pass. Do not submit while any test is failing.\n5. Submit with tool_dev_submit once all tests pass. This triggers human approval.\n\nGUARDRAILS:\n- Do NOT call tool_dev_submit until tool_dev_test returns all tests passed.\n- Minimum 2 test cases required before submission.\n- Keep the tool narrowly focused — one capability per tool.\n- Validate all inputs at the function boundary (check required fields, types, ranges).\n- Never embed secrets or credentials in the tool code.\n- Read shared facts first to check whether a similar tool already exists before creating a new one.\n- If a test keeps failing after 3 attempts with the same error hash, report the blocker instead of looping.",
      "tools": [
        "tool_dev_start",
        "tool_dev_test",
        "tool_dev_submit",
        "read_file",
        "write_file",
        "list_files",
        "workspace_search",
        "read_shared_facts",
        "share_finding"
      ]
    },
    "prompt_optimizer": {
      "description": "Reviews agent prompts for convergence, grounding, and tool-use quality. Proposes the smallest prompt change that improves routing fit, stopping behavior, and grounded output. Example queries: 'review the researcher agent prompt', 'optimize the coder system prompt to avoid infinite loops', 'prüfe den Systemprompt auf Konvergenz', 'Prompt-Qualität für den Prompt des data_analyst analysieren', 'the agent keeps looping, fix its prompt', 'Systemprompt überarbeiten damit er schneller zum Ziel kommt'.",
      "capabilities": ["prompt analysis", "Prompt-Analyse", "prompt rewriting", "Prompt-Umschreibung", "tool-use convergence", "Tool-Use-Konvergenz", "agent quality review", "Agenten-Qualitätsprüfung", "grounding improvement", "Verbesserung der Quellentreue", "stopping-condition tuning", "Abbruchbedingungs-Feintuning"],
      "tags": ["prompts", "prompt", "quality", "qualitaet", "agents", "agenten", "optimization", "optimierung", "convergence", "konvergenz", "tuning"],
      "model": {
        "primary": "lmstudio/qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b",
        "enableThinking": true,
        "temperature": 0.1,
        "maxTokens": 4096
      },
      "maxIterations": 8,
      "container": { "disabled": true }, // Tier 0/1 tools only — in-process is safe
      "systemPrompt": "You review prompts for routing fit, convergence, and grounding. Focus on whether the agent will stop at the right time, avoid repeating failures, and refuse unsupported claims. Propose the smallest prompt change that improves behavior.",
      "tools": [
        "read_file",
        "list_files",
        "read_shared_facts",
        "share_finding",
        "write_file"
      ]
    },
    "agent_architect": {
      "description": "Designs threshold-triggered ephemeral agents with the right prompt, tools, and model. Produces valid JSON agent specifications for one-off specialized tasks. Example queries: 'design a narrow agent for parsing receipts', 'entwirf einen Agenten für Börsenkurse', 'create an ephemeral agent for meeting-note extraction', 'Ad-hoc-Agent für die Übersetzung medizinischer Texte entwerfen', 'spec a specialist agent for CVE triage'.",
      "capabilities": ["agent design", "Agenten-Entwurf", "prompt writing", "Prompt-Erstellung", "model selection", "Modellauswahl", "ephemeral agents", "Ad-hoc-Agenten", "tool-set selection", "Toolset-Auswahl"],
      "tags": ["meta", "agents", "agenten", "architecture", "architektur", "design", "entwurf", "ephemeral", "adhoc"],
      "model": {
        "primary": "lmstudio/qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b",
        "enableThinking": true,
        "temperature": 0.1,
        "maxTokens": 4096
      },
      "maxIterations": 8,
      "container": { "disabled": true }, // pure read-only meta tools — in-process is safe
      "systemPrompt": "You design narrowly scoped agents for a single task. Return valid JSON only. Choose a concise role description, a grounded system prompt, a minimal tool set, and the most appropriate model override only when needed. Default all ephemeral agents to lmstudio/qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b. Only override downward to a smaller model when the task is trivially simple and token budget is a concern. Set enableThinking=true only when the agent must reconcile dense evidence or make difficult multi-step judgments; otherwise prefer enableThinking=false for faster tool execution. Never add tools that are not necessary for the task.",
      "tools": [
        "list_agents",
        "search_agents",
        "read_shared_facts"
      ]
    }
  }
}
